# Interaction latency, part 1 of 2. docs/perf/LATENCY.md has the budgets, the
# measurements, and why the unit is frames.
#
# Each arm is: arm the watcher on ink that is NOT on screen, do the input, read
# the wait. `watch_ink` refuses to arm on anything already present, and the
# clock starts when the input is consumed -- so a number here cannot be the
# runner's pacing and cannot be an always-present container.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}


require_thread t2
wait_frames 20

# 1. TYPING/SEARCH -- typing into the sidebar's search field, measured to the
#    clear affordance, which the app draws only once a query exists. A real
#    search path, and separate from the popup arm in part 2: this one filters a
#    list, that one builds an overlay.
click 140 58
wait_frames 6
watch_ink typing_search ui sb_search_clear
type "disk"
wait_frames 12
expect_latency typing_search 8

# 2. FOCUS -- an exact state transition; focus produces no new ink of its own,
#    so the watcher reads focus itself.
watch_ink focus focus composer_reply_input
click_ui composer_reply_input
wait_frames 6
expect_latency focus 5

# 3. SUBMIT -- Enter sends, measured to the assistant's acknowledgement, which
#    is what the person is actually waiting for. Not the sent text: the
#    composer is still drawing the draft when the watcher arms, and watch_ink
#    refuses to arm on something already on screen.
type "latency probe turn"
wait_frames 8
watch_ink submit text "Got it"
key ENTER
wait_frames 240
expect_latency submit 12

# Clear the query so the sidebar lists every thread again -- the arm below
# clicks a row the filter would otherwise hide.
click_ui sb_search_clear
wait_frames 10

# 4. CLICK TO FEEDBACK -- a sidebar row click, measured to a line only the
#    thread it opens contains.
watch_ink click_feedback text "412"
click_text "two workers still out"
wait_frames 14
expect_latency click_feedback 11
